      "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f64ffec6e5a5dc91\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nYet, many published exoplanets lack a measurement of their true mass, in particular those detected as a result of radial-velocity (RV) variations of their host star. For those examples, only the minimum mass, or m sin i, is known, owing to the insensitivity of RVs to the inclination of the detected orbit compared to the plane of the sky. The mass that is given in databases is generally that of an assumed edge-on system (~90°), but many other inclinations are possible, even extreme values closer to 0° (face-on).\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f64ffec6e5a5dc91\">>>",
